Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 14:17:04 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 095/106] Bluetooth: check the right bit for Write Synchronous
 Flow Control Enable

Octet 10 of the supported commands starts at Read Transmit Power Level
(0x0c2d), so Write Synchronous Flow Control Enable (0x0c2f) is bit 2.
Bit 4 is Host Buffer Size.

The only driver setting HCI_QUIRK_SYNC_FLOWCTL_SUPPORTED so far has been
hci_vhci, which claims every command, so the wrong bit never showed.

Checked against a WCN3990, which sets bit 2 and clears bit 4: asked by
hand it answers HCI_OP_WRITE_SYNC_FLOWCTL with success and then reports
synchronous flow control as enabled, while the kernel had never sent the
command.
---
 net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c | 13 +++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c b/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
index fd3aacdea..5c1dfcb8b 100644
--- a/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
+++ b/net/bluetooth/hci_sync.c
@@ -3868,8 +3868,17 @@ static int hci_write_sync_flowctl_sync(struct hci_dev *hdev)
 	struct hci_cp_write_sync_flowctl cp;
 	int err;
 
-	/* Check if the controller supports SCO and HCI_OP_WRITE_SYNC_FLOWCTL */
-	if (!lmp_sco_capable(hdev) || !(hdev->commands[10] & BIT(4)) ||
+	/*
+	 * Check if the controller supports SCO and HCI_OP_WRITE_SYNC_FLOWCTL.
+	 *
+	 * Octet 10 of the supported commands starts at Read Transmit Power
+	 * Level (0x0c2d), so Write Synchronous Flow Control Enable (0x0c2f) is
+	 * bit 2, not bit 4 - bit 4 is Host Buffer Size. The only user of the
+	 * quirk so far has been hci_vhci, which claims every command, so the
+	 * wrong bit never showed. A WCN3990 sets bit 2 and clears bit 4, and
+	 * answers HCI_OP_WRITE_SYNC_FLOWCTL with success.
+	 */
+	if (!lmp_sco_capable(hdev) || !(hdev->commands[10] & BIT(2)) ||
 	    !hci_test_quirk(hdev, HCI_QUIRK_SYNC_FLOWCTL_SUPPORTED))
 		return 0;
 
